  "name": "Azerbaijani mugham",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Azerbaijani classical modal art-music tradition of the modal-improvisatory dastgah suite system. The seven-dastgah canonical sequence — Rast, Shur, Segah, Chargah, Bayati-Shiraz, Shushtar, Humayun — each suite built from a tonal-center microtonal modal vocabulary with characteristic shifted-third intervals, extended through gushe sub-section sequences. Khananda singer-led trio with tar long-necked plucked lute (eleven-or-twelve-string mulberry-wood double-bowl body, redesigned by Sadigjan in the 1870s into its modern form) and kamancha four-string spike fiddle, with the khananda holding a gaval frame-drum. The Shusha \"conservatory of the East\" lineage established the Garabagh school as the canonical performance dialect. Twentieth-century founding figures include Jabbar Garyaghdioglu (1861-1944), Khan Shushinski (1901-1979), Seyid Shushinski, with Uzeyir Hajibeyov (1885-1948) founding the symphonic mugham and the 1908 opera Leyli and Majnun. Distinguished from Iraqi maqam by Garabagh-Shusha dialect-specific gushe-sequence conventions; distinguished from Persian radif by Azerbaijani-Turkic vocal-ornamentation grammar; distinguished from Turkish makam by the dastgah modal-cycle structural framework",
